      "marks": 4,
      "answer": "$x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$.",
      "official_mark_scheme_text": "Use the correct product rule to obtain $\\frac{d}{d\\theta}(\\pm2\\cos^3\\theta\\sin\\theta)$ — M1. Condone incorrect naming of the derivative; the work must be in the correct context.\n\nObtain the correct derivative in any form — A1, e.g. $\\pm(-2\\cos^4\\theta+6\\sin^2\\theta\\cos^2\\theta)$.\n\nEquate the derivative to zero and obtain an equation in one trigonometric ratio — A1, e.g. $3\\tan^2\\theta=1$, $4\\sin^2\\theta=1$, or $4\\cos^2\\theta=3$.\n\nObtain $x=-1/\\sqrt3$ — A1. Or $-\\sqrt3/3$.\n\nAlternative method: use the correct quotient rule to obtain $d^2y/dx^2$ — M1; obtain the correct derivative in any form — A1, $[-2(1+x^2)^2+2(2x)(2x)(1+x^2)]/(1+x^2)^4$; equate the derivative to zero and obtain an equation in $x^2$ — A1, e.g. $6x^2=2$; obtain $x=-1/\\sqrt3$ — A1.\n\nTotal [4].",
